   "text": "without having done this topic. The mistake 1 is peeking, which means you will check results before the required sample is reached, and then you will feel, \"Hey, the number look good. Let's stop the test and let's continue with whatever we have learned from this particular test.\" Every time you peek at immediate results, you inflate the false positive rate. Now, what we mean by false positive? Let's say, there is variant A and variant B. There is 10% difference or maybe let's say, only 1% difference. But in the first 100 entries, that 1% difference goes up to 10% difference rate. And the team thinks, \"Oh, we thought there's a difference of 10%,\" and they decided on that basis. Wherein, if they would have continued for 900 more test, that would have shown that no, the difference is not 10 and just 1%. So, you actually have to set your sample size in advance itself, so that you do not open the results unless you hit it. The mistake 2, setting your MDE too low because then you actually have to go for a very, very high sample size, and that can take a very high amount of timeframe. So, the fix is you set MDE based on what would actually change your decision, which is a more practical 10, 15 or 20% kind of bracket, as we discussed. The mistake 3 is forgetting that sample size is a variant. Let's say, if your calculator tells you or we get that calculation from an AI calculator, that 560 visitors are required, that is 560 per variant."
