   "text": "and then expand our operations or marketing or acquisition spend only for these 22% kind of people? So, that's what your cohort analysis helps you with. So, you would not grow the vanity metric. The vanity metric means the top 8,400, which looks good, but it's not of use. You will always focus on the North Star metric, as we said in our leading versus lagging metric discussion. North Star metric is always more important. So, those 22% people who are staying with you after 3 months is your North Star metric, let's say. So, you don't have to work on your growing your vanity metric rather, you will focus on growing your North Star metric, because that is where the actual problem should get solved. If you increase your vanity metric, it will get even worse. So, the right call is to fix activation and retention first instead of going forward on the acquisition. And you can only see that call clearly when you look at the cohort data. Now, let's look at Zoko with a different version of the same insight. Suppose, look at aggregate number. They have 6,200 all-time customers. 480 customers they get every month. Looks good. But let's say now segment by behavior, and then 2 completely different intentions appear. One-time buyers, so they have one-time buyers. Month one repurchase rate is 22%. 78% do not come back within 30 days."
