   "lectureId": "BRGSA-M04-L01",
   "title": "Growth Marketing and Product",
   "text": "And the time horizon would be one of the longest because product is a long-term game. A roadmap plays over, let's say, multiple of quarters, an annual vision or 2 years vision or 3 years vision. The methods a product guy could use would be user research, roadmap prioritization, feature discovery, user interface, design, engineering, analytics. So, building and shaping the product or the business use case. The product's job ends when the feature or the product is shipped and people start using it. Whether the business grows because of it is, again, someone else's problem. A product can be technically excellent, but maybe from the user perspective or revenue perspective, it is irrelevant. But sometimes the commercial viability of that product is very well communicated to the end user. So, who exactly owns that? Now, the product scorecard answers one question: Did we build a good product? Is the business growing because of it? It is a different question and again with a different owner. Now, which brings us to growth. And growth is that third function, not marketing under a trendy name, not product analytics. It has its own discipline with its own job. And its job is to move specific business metrics by running experiments across the complete customer funnel, connecting acquisition to activation, activation to retention, retention to revenue, and revenue to referral. And that's how the entire AARR loop is seen as a system."
