   "title": "Growth Marketing and Product",
   "text": "For Clairo, the user receiving their first auto-generated follow-up email would be the aha moment. And then the growth person would actually say that, \"Hey, how long does it take to get there?\" Suppose, 14 minutes from sign up, which is too long at this point of time. So now, growth will run three activation experiments in parallel. Let's say, first they will do that, hey, let's launch a 5-minute guided first meeting path. That how you can actually get the first meeting. The second experiment they will run is they will have a pre-populated demo meeting. And the third, they might try something like, let's say, remove the four-step journey entirely so that it becomes very easy. Now, each one of this is might be, let's say, 1 to 2 weeks a long experiment with a clear success metric. And then whichever experiment out of these three lifts the first week recording rate, you know that, okay, fine, this works, and then you just ship that specific feature or that path. Now, all three responses are reasonable. Only one targets the right metric at the right speed. That 66% gap is an activation problem, and the growth will own it. And here is the math behind why it matters. Let's say Clairo's current payback period is 8.5 months, and their activation sits at 34%. Suppose, if they lift their activation from 34% to 50%, their payback period might compress under 6 months."
