   "title": "Channel market Fit",
   "text": "or the average contract value in B2B terms, as we just said or if you say in B2C terms, it is average order value, which is called as AOV. So suppose if your product costs 999 rupees a month, and your channel CAC for that specific channel has to be under 3,200 to keep the economics workable. Now, that rules out most paid channels at the start because initially, your unit economics might not be so good. Now suppose if your product costs 50,000 rupees every company, then you can afford much more expensive channels. Let's say even if you're actually getting a 20,000 rupees CAC, that'll be fine because you're already earning 50,000 rupees from the customer. So, your price point is the first filter from your product side, and generally this gets skipped because we feel that, okay, this channel is okay, but then you also have to feel that does this work with my unit economics or not? Otherwise, you will end up burning cash. The second you will have to understand what is the purchase frequency of your own given product. Is it a one-time buyer product or is it something like SaaS where your customer is going to pay month on month? So, suppose if your CAC is 3,200 and your monthly cost or the customer cost is, let's say 1,000, but suppose your customer pays to you for at least 10 months, so you're earning 10,000 rupees. A CAC of 3,200 would work fine. But suppose if it is a one-time cost, then it is going to be a loss because customer pays 1,000."
