   "title": "Impact Confidence and Ease ICE Prioritisation",
   "text": "It might create very small minor changes, but not a real change unless and until Clairo's growth team or the whole product team works on improving their primary constraint, which is activation. Now, look at the top row. If you cut onboarding from, let's say, 4 steps to 2, the impact is 9 because you know it directly attacks the activation constraint. Confidence is 8 because they already have the data showing that each extra step cost or like is actually creating a huge downfall or a loss of users at a measurable rate. Ease is 7 because, of course, it requires real product work, but the measurement is already in place. And you can see they have an ICE score of 8.0, and that's why it is top of the list. If you look at row two, here they are talking about an experiment, let's say, let's default the auto record toggle button to on for the next time after sign-up. Here, they have scored impact an 8 because this also supports activation. Confidence at 7 because there is comparable evidence from other pr, products or other freemium SaaS products. Ease is 6 because, of course, it requires a little more effort, and its ICE score is 7.0. Row three, prebuilt meeting templates by the type of sales call. Again, activation, and accordingly, you can see the score. Their ICE is 7.3. Row four, you can see that it is talking about new homepage hero copy AB test. Impact only 5 because this is supporting acquisition and not activation, which is actually the primary constraint for Clairo."
