   "citation": "BRGSA-M08-L01#18",
   "lectureId": "BRGSA-M08-L01",
   "title": "Impact Confidence and Ease ICE Prioritisation",
   "text": "Suppose, when a similar experiment fails, you rescore the related items in your backlog sheet. So, the framework only works if the scores update with any new evidence and with the changing or with the change of the customer or any kind of data that you're getting. Now, here is one more diagnostic that will tell you in, let's say, in 30 seconds whether your team is scoring honestly or not. Look at the, like the spread of your ICE scores on your backlog sheet. If you see that, hey, most of your backlog is between 7 and 9, then it is clear that your team is not scoring honestly because a real backlog sheet should actually have a diversified range. Some ideas might have an ICE score of 8 or 9, some might have 4 to 6, some might have 2 or 3, and that diversification is actually that makes the prioritization meaningful. So, a backlog sheet where everything is a 7 is a backlog where the framework has actually been failed or done in a wrong way. Now, let us bring this together. 5 takeaways. First, ICE is impact plus confidence plus ease averaged"
