 "ibm_bop_short_answer": [
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M01-L07#03",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M01-L07",
   "title": "Bottom of the Pyramid Markets",
   "text": "days, at least there weren't many rich people in these countries which the multinationals were planning to target, and hence those markets will be very, very small. Whereas if they can really target the broader markets, people who were at the bottom of the pyramid, then those markets were very big. And that's why he started building an argument. He said that if you can design your product and service in a right manner, then you can make a fortune in the bottom of the pyramid markets. That's how the terminology fortune at the bottom of the pyramid became very popular. He also wrote a paper, and then subsequently he wrote a book with the same title. By fortune, he meant that multinational companies can make a profit by selling their products and services to these people who were at the bottom of the pyramid. Now start linking this to what we were talking about earlier in terms of inclusive business models. Moment we are talking of bottom of the pyramid, you can think of people who are not very rich, some of them who are poor. And he was advising multinational enterprises, so he was not talking about making a charity or making a donation. He was saying that you can take advantage of the markets, if you create the suitable products and services, then you can make profit in those markets. So quite like Muhammad Yunus, he was also arguing that it is possible to address the needs of the poor and yet make profits in those markets."
  },
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M01-L07#08",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M01-L07",
   "title": "Bottom of the Pyramid Markets",
   "text": "He was very encouraging in his view about saying that all these companies must go to these developing economies, give their products and services, which are usually very good, but make fundamental innovations to address these markets. He said that merely removing some bells and whistles from the product is not going to help you because then you are not going to address the needs of this particular market. Now, this certainly got the boardrooms of these multinational enterprises very excited, and many of them started talking about how you can address the people who are at the bottom of the pyramid and yet make profits out of that. In tune with what Muhammad Yunus was already saying or what we have learned so far, that is there a possibility of addressing the needs of the poor in a financially sustainable manner. Now, having heard about especially what CK Prahalad was talking, read the Diageo case that we are going to share with you and try to answer some of the following questions. First of all, look at all the innovations that Diageo did to address the bottom of the pyramid market in Kenya. They were trying to sell low cost beer to this particular market. And just as I told you, Diageo is a multinational enterprise who are now trying to get into the market, especially thinking that can I address the drinking needs of the poor people in Kenya? Think of all the factors that contributed to them creating a successful business."
  },
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M01-L07#02",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M01-L07",
   "title": "Bottom of the Pyramid Markets",
   "text": "And he said that this is largely the population which resides in those developing economy markets which the multinationals want to go. Markets like India, China, Brazil, Russia and South Africa. But he said that, well, the multinationals are not very well suited with their products and services to get into these markets. Why? Because they have been always used to selling to reasonably rich people, especially people who would be really called rich if they were residing in some of these countries. So that is where he came up with this term called the bottom of the pyramid. It simply means bottom of the economic pyramid. And he encouraged the multinationals to target those people rather than confining their products to selling only to the rich people. Because the challenge was that in those"
  },
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M01-L07#07",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M01-L07",
   "title": "Bottom of the Pyramid Markets",
   "text": "Brazil, Russia, South America, or South Africa, they had certain challenges. There may be access problems, infrastructure would not be very well developed. The financial services are not so suitable that people can easily take credit. Information may not be available widespread. So he said that if multinational enterprises wanted to break into these markets, they also need to invest in providing the right information. Sometimes they have to create some infrastructure, and they have to constantly innovate. So the message that he gave to the world was that, yes there is a possibility to make profit in these markets. But to make those profits, you have to constantly innovate. Because many of these markets have inherent structural limitations, and your business needs to address those. So those messages of encouraging people to be innovative, to be entrepreneurial, was also very important for us to understand how one can create profitable businesses by addressing the needs of the poor. CK also highlighted the fact that many of these countries are also embracing market forces. They were earlier probably not very democratic, not embracing market forces, very, very regulated but many of these markets are opening up, embracing market forces, which naturally enables multinational companies to sell their products and services. So he was very optimistic."
  },
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M01-L07#01",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M01-L07",
   "title": "Bottom of the Pyramid Markets",
   "text": "The second person whom we need to talk about when we are talking about inclusive business model is Dr. C.K. Prahalad. Dr. Prahalad was a professor in the Michigan University in their business school, and he was very well known as a strategy professor because of his influential paper, which he had written in early 90s called the Core Competency of Organization. Because he was very famous, he was appointed as an advisor to many of the American companies. Now, these multinational companies towards the end of the last century, realized that their traditional markets which was North America, Western Europe, typically the developed markets were getting saturated. Those places were no longer growing. Whereas the growth markets were typically Brazil, Russia, India, China and South Africa. We even created a terminology in those days called the BRICS. Now, these multinational enterprises came and asked Dr. Prahalad that what strategy they need to adopt to break into this BRICS market or typically markets which were of developing nations, nations which were not as rich as their traditional markets but which seem to be having a lot of people and lot of demand. Now, CK as he was very well known, apparently made a back of the envelope calculation, and he said that world over, at that point of time, there were 4 billion people who were living under earning $1500 a year annually."
  },
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M01-L07#06",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M01-L07",
   "title": "Bottom of the Pyramid Markets",
   "text": "But what Arvind had figured out was that if you can do your cataract surgeries very efficiently, then you can generate some profit by treating the rich patients, and whatever profit you generate can be used to treat the patients, to treat the poor patients. So he was citing all of these examples to say that if you can structure your products and services in a particular manner, you can address these very large markets. But he also warned that, remember, here you cannot sell your products and services at a very high price. So the per unit profitability of these products will be very low, will be very at the margin. But because you are selling in thousands or millions, your total profitability would be quite large, quite significant, which excited many of those companies to look at these markets. Now, if you go deeper into CK Prahalad's arguments, he also said that these developing economy markets, typically"
  }
 ],
