   "text": "to try through LabourNet. The founder, Gayatri, was always very careful that she wanted to create an organization which is financially sustainable. And that's why LabourNet is of great interest to our course, because in this course, we are constantly trying to look at organizations, look at examples, look at case studies, who are able to balance both these objectives. Am I inclusive? Am I addressing the needs of the poor? And yet, am I being able to financially sustain myself? The good news is that finally, after all of these struggles, LabourNet was actually able to become financially sustainable. And so their journey, their struggles, their failures, and eventually their success should have good lessons for us. When LabourNet started their business, they understood that there were lot of these informal sector workers who spend time in searching for jobs. While they have skills, they are not always aware that where is that place where that skill is needed. There may be several people in a large residential complex who needs a plumber, but how does a person who comes from a village with some plumbing capabilities know that some household in some place in Bangalore will actually need my service on a particular day? So, LabourNet wanted to create some kind of platform, some kind of market where you can connect the service providers with points of service demand. Now, in order to do that, one of the most important things is to create a database of informal sector workers."
