   "lectureId": "IBM-M07-L04",
   "title": "Understanding the FPO Journey",
   "text": "This is where maybe they can sell low methane emission rice, low residue produce for infant cereal products. Unless and until they are able to make their products distinctive, they will not be able to compete with a trader in the open market. Because remember, a trader has the advantage of larger volumes. He or she typically has more money, so he can buy low and sell high. But what the trader cannot do is influence the process of production or the quality of harvest, which an FPO can do. An FPO can look at a very distinctive product and say that, \"My rice has low methane emission,\" or, \"My turmeric has been grown in a manner that it is very organic in nature.\" And organizations such as KissanSe can actually certify that. So in general, our impression is that FPOs are much stronger if they're able to sell products which are very distinctive in nature, not really selling products which are very generic. And it's a very good sign that there are a lot of other startups in this domain who are providing various services to the FPOs, such as warehousing, financing as well as digital marketing. And if any of you are thinking about building a company which will work with the farmer producer organizations, then I think you can think in similar lines. You are not a farmer. I am not probably expecting you to join an FPO, but you are creating an organization which can do this mediation between markets and the farmer producer organizations."
