   "title": "Understanding Impact Investment",
   "text": "One of the other things I mentioned was development impact bonds. This is an example of what a development impact bond is. Very simply, what it really means is that there is an investor who's willing to put in a capital for a nonprofit activity, but the investor expects a return to be generated only if the non-profit activity results in the impact it is promising to do. To simplify it, and I'll, I'll talk you through this example quickly. In this case, there is a non-profit NGO called Educate Girls, whose objective is to improve education for girls. There are a few specific parameters that were determined for that. The investor in this case was a foundation called UBS Optimus Foundation, which gave some money to Educate Girls for their programs. If Educate Girls is able to generate that impact that it promised, then there was another entity, which is a philanthropic entity, which promised to pay UBS Optimus the investor, a certain sum of money. But UBS Optimus is taking the risk that the impact will be delivered. So, if the impact is delivered, then they get their returns. If the impact is not delivered, they do not get their returns. So, it directly links an impact return to a financial return. In this specific case, this was a highly successful placement, and the investor actually made a fairly strong return of 15% in dollar terms, which is fairly attractive."
